MedLens

Build a complete, working, demo-ready web app called MedLens. Follow this brief exactly. Where something is ambiguous, choose the simplest option that works. Do not ask me questions. Make the app mobile-first (it must look like a native mobile app on a phone) and fully usable on desktop.

1. APP OVERVIEW

App name: MedLens

One-line description: Scan a medicine package and instantly understand what it is, how to store it, when it expires, and whether it clashes with your other medicines, explained in simple language (English, Tamil, Hindi, Telugu).

Core problem: Medicine packaging and leaflets are full of small print, technical terms and unfamiliar ingredient names. People confuse medicines, miss expiry dates, forget storage rules, and accidentally take two products with the same active ingredient under different brand names (for example Dolo 650 and Combiflam both contain paracetamol).

Target users:

Elderly people managing several prescriptions.
Caregivers and family members managing medicines for parents and children.
Students and ordinary people buying over-the-counter medicines.

Positioning (use this wording in the UI and README): MedLens is not another reminder app. It is an intelligent interface between complicated pharmaceutical information and ordinary people.

Core concept shown on the landing page: Scan → Recognise → Understand → Organise → Stay Informed.

NON-NEGOTIABLE SAFETY RULES (enforce in code, prompts and UI):

The AI never invents medical facts. It only explains information from the verified record shown in the app (local curated DB, openFDA, or RxNorm) and always cites the source.
The AI never gives dosage instructions, diagnoses, treatment recommendations, or prescription changes. Personal medical questions get this reply: “I can’t advise on that. Please ask your pharmacist or doctor.”
A warning is not a diagnosis. Every interaction or duplicate warning ends with “Please review this with your pharmacist or doctor.”
When OCR or matching is uncertain, ask the user to confirm. Never guess silently.
A persistent footer on every page: “MedLens is an information and organisation tool, not a medical decision-maker. It does not replace a pharmacist or doctor.”
