6.3 Seed: glossary (used for popovers and fallback explanations)

Analgesic = Medicine used to relieve pain. Antipyretic = Medicine used to reduce fever. Contraindication = A situation in which a medicine should not be used. Side effects = Unwanted effects that may occur. Active ingredient = The substance responsible for the medicine’s intended action. NSAID = A pain and inflammation reliever that can irritate the stomach. PPI = A medicine that reduces stomach acid. Antihistamine = Medicine that eases allergy symptoms. Antiplatelet = Medicine that makes blood less likely to clot. Gastro-resistant = A coating that protects the tablet from stomach acid. Batch number = A code that identifies the production lot of the medicine. Provide each term in English, Tamil, Hindi and Telugu (generate accurate translations).

6.4 Seed: curated interactions (ingredient-level, symmetric)
ibuprofen + aspirin: known, moderate. Taking them together can raise the risk of stomach bleeding, and ibuprofen may reduce the heart-protective effect of low-dose aspirin.
ibuprofen + diclofenac: same_class, high. Both are NSAIDs. Taking two together increases the risk of stomach and kidney problems.
diclofenac + aspirin: known, moderate. Higher chance of bleeding or stomach irritation.
ibuprofen + telmisartan and diclofenac + telmisartan: known, moderate. NSAIDs may weaken the blood-pressure-lowering effect and can strain the kidneys.
omeprazole + clopidogrel: known, high. Omeprazole may reduce how well clopidogrel works.
aspirin + clopidogrel: known, info. Together they raise bleeding risk. Doctors sometimes prescribe them together on purpose, so do not stop either without medical advice.
pantoprazole + omeprazole: same_class, moderate. Both reduce stomach acid.
cetirizine + fexofenadine: same_class, moderate. Both are antihistamines, and taking two may cause extra drowsiness or side effects.

Duplicate rule (computed, not stored): if two cabinet items share any identical ingredient name, raise a “Duplicate active ingredient” alert (severity high). The explanation must name both brands and the shared ingredient, for example “Dolo 650 and Combiflam both contain paracetamol, so using both could mean taking more paracetamol than intended.”

