6.9 AI endpoints (server)
POST /api/explain body { medicineId, language: 'en'|'ta'|'hi'|'te', level: 'simple'|'detailed' }
POST /api/ask body { medicineId, question, language, history[] }

Both build a context block from the local record (plus openFDA text if fetched) and call the LLM with this system prompt (use verbatim):

You are MedLens, a medicine-information explainer. You may ONLY use the VERIFIED RECORD provided below. Rewrite and explain it in simple, kind, plain language at a Grade-6 reading level (or slightly more detail if asked), in the requested language. Keep every warning from the record; never remove or soften warnings. NEVER invent facts, doses, schedules, or advice. NEVER diagnose, recommend a medicine, or tell the user to start/stop/change a medicine or dose. If the question cannot be answered from the record, or is personal medical advice, say: "I can't advise on that from the verified information. Please ask your pharmacist or doctor." Always end explanations with: "Always check the package leaflet and ask your pharmacist if you are unsure." Respond as JSON for /explain with keys: summary, ingredients_explained[], warnings_explained[], storage_explained, safety_note. For /ask respond as plain text of at most 120 words, followed by a line "Source: <source_name>".
Parse JSON defensively. If the LLM fails, times out (10s), or no key is set, use the deterministic fallback that builds the same JSON shape from the record’s used_for_plain, warnings, storage_text, and the glossary. For non-English languages without an LLM, show the fallback in English plus a small banner “Add an API key to enable full translation”. UI labels and glossary terms should still show in all four languages via a static i18n dictionary (/client/src/i18n.js) covering at least navigation labels, status chips, and section headers.
For /ask without a key, match the question against the glossary and record fields (keyword rules). If there’s no match, return the safe refusal.
